68
00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:35.940
Sentence E (this is the last one).

69
00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:44.280
For the last two days in the challenge, 

70
00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:46.680
I've asked you to remember
the sentences. 

71
00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:54.419
There are many links between memory and hearing.
I've written a blog article on this topic, 

72
00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:56.940
and I'll link to it below
in the description section. 

73
00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:01.440
But today, I want to talk specifically
about working memory. 

74
00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:06.360
You use working memory,
for example, when you hear a phone number 

75
00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:08.759
and you want to hold on to it
for a little while until 

76
00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:12.780
you can find a pen to write it down.
Or if you're following a recipe, 

77
00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:16.800
and you have to remember:
half a cup of sugar, half a cup of sugar. 

78
00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:20.699
When you partly hear something,
maybe part of a word 

79
00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:23.160
or part of one word
and part of another word, 

80
00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:28.980
you have to hold on to that fragment
with your auditory working memory. 

81
00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:37.080
You hold on to that fragment
so that you can do an internal database search 

82
00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:41.220
in your own mind
to find something that fits. 

83
00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:45.060
So you're thinking,
what does that sound like? 

84
00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:50.099
You may have noticed
that sometimes when you don't hear clearly, 

85
00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:52.500
if you have a moment
to think about it 

86
00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:55.979
then you can figure it out.
And that's because 

87
00:06:55.980 --> 00:06:58.592
of that database search.

88
00:06:58.592 --> 00:07:02.178
Your working memory has to be strong enough

89
00:07:02.178 --> 00:07:04.680
to hold on to these fragments.

90
00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:07.860
By the way,
you can strengthen 

91
00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:11.879
your auditory working memory
through auditory training programs, 

92
00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:14.798
which is another passion of mine.

93
00:07:14.798 --> 00:07:19.087
This kind of memory work is heavy in terms of

94
00:07:19.087 --> 00:07:21.085
consuming brain resources.

95
00:07:21.227 --> 00:07:23.581
And if you're having to do this

96
00:07:23.581 --> 00:07:25.688
while also listening, it's going to be hard

97
00:07:25.688 --> 00:07:30.496
to do anything else,
like smile and be witty.

98
00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:35.700
So your facial expression
might look something more like this.

99
00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:42.198
It can be exhausting.

100
00:07:42.198 --> 00:07:45.661
I mention this because it's important to be aware

101
00:07:45.661 --> 00:07:47.640
that listening is hard work,

102
00:07:48.540 --> 00:07:53.520
especially if you have underamplified
or untreated hearing loss. 

103
00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:57.660
But even with hearing aids,
you're totally multitasking 

104
00:07:57.660 --> 00:08:00.300
when you're listening
and remembering 

105
00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:02.940
and reading lips
and trying to 

106
00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:05.580
separate the voices from the background noise. 

107
00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:09.840
Lipreading and hearing aids
are another two for one. 

108
00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:14.220
Both hearing aids and lipreading
can support you. 

109
00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:20.160
It's almost like having a hard drive
with two databases in it: 

110
00:08:20.820 --> 00:08:27.240
a database for what it sounds like,
and another database for what it looks like.

111
00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:33.240
So if you have hearing aids
without lip reading or vice versa, 

112
00:08:33.240 --> 00:08:36.180
it's almost like having a smaller hard drive.

113
00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:42.539
It takes time and energy
on many levels 

114
00:08:42.540 --> 00:08:49.079
to invest in your lip reading skills,
or technology, if you need technology. 

115
00:08:50.340 --> 00:08:52.140
I just want to acknowledge that, 

116
00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:54.691
because it may seem daunting.

117
00:08:54.691 --> 00:08:55.620
It's worth the effort

118
00:08:56.580 --> 00:09:00.060
because communication
is at the heart of connecting to other people. 

119
00:09:01.260 --> 00:09:04.680
And through this lip reading challenge,
I'm trying to show you places 

120
00:09:04.680 --> 00:09:07.080
where you can put your effort
to make things better. 

121
00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:11.700
That's it for today!
I hope it was helpful. 

122
00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:18.180
